2026-05-02T15:05:00Z design-questions-surfaced:
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q1-node-id-scheme: Radar uses snake-cased author-assigned strings ("collect_items", "fetch_<source_id>", "render_html"); admin-pack §9 same shape ("intake_scan", "compose_bundle"). Plan.to_map serialises node_id verbatim. v1.0.0 options: (a) any non-empty string; (b) snake_case lowercase regex (matches both Radar + bookkeeping shapes today); (c) no constraint. Lean (a)/(b). Bookkeeping has "fetch_${source_id}" which is dynamically built — if source_id is "rss-1" you'd get "fetch_rss-1" which fails strict snake_case. Radar's fetch_${source[id]} = "fetch_<id>" where id is author-controlled. STRONG LEAN: (a) string non-empty + length cap (1..63 same as pack_id), no regex constraint. The dynamic builder argues against tight constraint.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q2-op-reference-shape: A plan-node references an op by op_module: atom. Plan.to_map renders as Atom.to_string(module) -> "Elixir.Liminara.Radar.Ops.FetchRss". This is Elixir-internal; downstream Python SDK or admin-pack proxy can't author such strings without leaking Elixir module convention. Options: (a) freeze "Elixir.<...>" as the literal contract (Radar-shaped, leaks Elixir-isms); (b) bare op-name as string ("fetch_rss") matching ExecutionSpec.identity.name; (c) two-field: pack_id + op_name; (d) defer — schema admits the Elixir-prefixed shape today, breaking change later. Lean (b) — bare op-name matching identity.name. Radar's op_module->op-name correspondence is mechanical (FetchRss<->radar_fetch_rss); a YAML-renderer at M-RUNTIME-02 can do that mapping. This makes plan portable across language SDKs without Elixir leak. RISK: Plan.to_map currently emits Elixir-prefixed string — schema would NOT validate Plan.to_map output today; we'd be saying 'the contract YAML form is what M-RUNTIME-02 renders, not what to_map emits today'. That's a real gap to surface for Q&A.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q3-inputs-binding-shape: Plan.to_map serialises inputs as map of {name: {type: 'literal'|'ref', ...}}. Three concrete shapes: literal {type:literal, value:<string>}, ref {type:ref, ref:<id>}, ref-with-key {type:ref, ref:<id>, key:<key>}. Admin-pack adds {:init, key}. Options for v1.0.0: (a) freeze the three Radar shapes only (literal+ref+ref-with-key) and reject :init; (b) admit init as fourth optional shape now; (c) ban literal inputs entirely (declarative plans should bind only to refs/init — literals are runtime-injected). Lean (a). Init-binding is admin-pack specific and 1.0 is allowed to land Radar-only per parent-epic 'lock to actually-used set, expand additively'. This forces admin-pack to drive the v1.1.0 additive bump for {:init} and pressure-test the shape from a real consumer.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q4-literal-value-encoding: Plan.to_map serialises {:literal, v} as {type:literal, value: inspect(v)} — Elixir inspect format ('"hello"' for strings, '%{...}' for maps). That's NOT portable: Python SDK can't author 'inspect'-formatted strings. Options: (a) freeze inspect-formatted strings (Radar-shaped today, language-leak); (b) require JSON-encoded value (Jason.encode); (c) require raw YAML scalar/structure value. Lean (b)/(c). RISK: same as Q2 — Plan.to_map's current output would not be valid against the schema. The contract here is what M-RUNTIME-02 RENDERS, not what to_map currently does.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q5-ref-output-key-required-or-optional: ref shape has two flavours: {:ref, id} (whole-output) and {:ref, id, key} (specific output). Schema options: (a) require key always (force authors to be explicit); (b) optional key (preserve both shapes); (c) require key, drop the no-key shape (force explicit). Lean (b). Radar uses both ({:ref, "compose_briefing", "briefing"} with key, {:ref, node_id} without key in earlier walks). Both are live shapes today. Don't tighten beyond what Radar actually uses.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q6-plan-id-and-version: Should the plan itself carry id/name/version? Radar's plan/1 returns a Plan with no plan-level identity fields — it's the runtime DAG and its identity is the run identity. Pack already has pack_id+pack_version. Lean: NO plan-level identity. Adding it now reaches into admin-pack-shaped territory (multiple plans per pack — ADR-MULTIPLAN-01 deferred). v1.0.0 is single-plan per pack with no plan-level identity.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q7-multi-plan-block: ADR-MULTIPLAN-01 is M-CONTRACT-04 (deferred). Options: (a) v1.0.0 schema is strictly one plan per YAML document, no plans:[...] block, multi-plan is breaking v2.0.0; (b) admit plans:[...] now even though only one is used; (c) reserve a top-level 'plan_set?:' field. Lean (a). Same as Q3 — pressure must come from real consumer (admin-pack period plans, file-watch trigger plans). v1.0.0 = single plan = one document. MULTIPLAN-01 will land additively or breakingly when authored.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q8-conditional-or-static: Radar's plan today is fully static — every node lands unconditionally. Admin-pack §9 shows `enabled: input.use_llm` as a per-node kw passed alongside inputs (not a binding, a runtime gating flag). Options: (a) v1.0.0 strict static — no conditional/skip flags admitted; (b) admit `enabled: bool` at node level optional; (c) admit a richer condition shape. Lean (a). Admin-pack's `enabled:` is a forward-looking shape; freezing it without the actual runtime semantics would over-commit. v1.0.0 is static-DAG-only.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q9-deps-derivation: Schema design choice — does the schema require an explicit `deps: [node_id]` field per node OR derive deps from the inputs map's :ref bindings? Radar today derives (no separate deps list — Plan.check_dangling_refs walks inputs.values to extract refs). Options: (a) implicit-deps via inputs:ref bindings only (Radar-faithful); (b) explicit deps:[] field plus inputs (redundant, allows assertion); (c) explicit deps required + inputs only carries values. Lean (a). Plan.to_map output has no `deps:` field — adding it for the YAML form would diverge from the live Elixir shape and break round-trip.
2026-05-02T15:05:00Z Q10-top-level-schema-version: All M-CONTRACT-02 schemas (op-execution-spec, wire-protocol, replay-protocol, manifest) have SOME notion of schema_version. Plan needs the same field per parent-epic spec? Spec says ADR-MANIFEST-01 specifies the schema_version field — plans are sub-data carried inside the manifest's ops list per ADR-MANIFEST-01, but a free-standing pack.yaml-companion plan.yaml would need its own. Lean: top-level schema_version: int >=1, required, mirroring MANIFEST. The schema_version field at MANIFEST level constrains the manifest; plans rendered separately need their own field. This applies if M-RUNTIME-02 renders plans as a separate plan.yaml; if it renders embedded in pack.yaml the manifest's field covers it. SURFACE FOR Q&A: do we render plans embedded in pack.yaml or as separate plan.yaml? If embedded, drop top-level schema_version.
